/* Pharmacology 2–3: ANS, autacoids, NSAIDs, respiratory */
Z("pharm2",0,"ph",["Cholinergic drugs mimic acetylcholine; they act directly on receptors (choline esters, alkaloids) or indirectly by blocking the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine (anticholinesterases).","Anticholinesterases don't ring the bell themselves; they stop the bell from being silenced, so acetylcholine keeps ringing longer.",["Direct: choline esters (ACh, methacholine, carbachol, bethanechol); alkaloids (pilocarpine, muscarine, arecoline).","Reversible anticholinesterases: physostigmine (tertiary — crosses BBB), neostigmine, pyridostigmine, edrophonium, rivastigmine, donepezil, galantamine.","Irreversible: organophosphates (parathion, malathion, sarin), carbamates (carbaryl, propoxur), echothiophate.","Uses: myasthenia gravis (neostigmine, pyridostigmine), reversal of NM block (neostigmine), atropine poisoning (physostigmine), glaucoma, postoperative urinary retention/paralytic ileus, Alzheimer's (donepezil), cobra bite."],"Physostigmine = 'Physically crosses BBB' (tertiary)."],
"Cholinergic (parasympathomimetic) drugs produce effects similar to acetylcholine. Classification: (A) direct-acting — choline esters: acetylcholine, methacholine, carbachol, bethanechol; alkaloids: pilocarpine, muscarine, arecoline; (B) indirect-acting (anticholinesterases) — reversible: carbamates — physostigmine (tertiary amine, natural, crosses BBB), neostigmine, pyridostigmine, edrophonium (short-acting), ambenonium, rivastigmine, donepezil, galantamine (centrally acting, Alzheimer disease); irreversible: organophosphates — dyflos (DFP), echothiophate, parathion, malathion, diazinon, tabun, sarin, soman (nerve gases); carbamate insecticides — carbaryl, propoxur. Mechanism: anticholinesterases inhibit acetylcholinesterase (and butyrylcholinesterase), preventing hydrolysis of ACh, thus amplifying its action at muscarinic, nicotinic (ganglionic and neuromuscular) and central sites. Reversible agents carbamylate the enzyme (hydrolysed in hours); organophosphates phosphorylate it, and ageing makes it irreversible. Neostigmine also has direct action on nicotinic NM receptors. Uses of anticholinesterases: myasthenia gravis — diagnosis (edrophonium/Tensilon test; ice-pack test) and treatment (pyridostigmine, neostigmine); reversal of non-depolarising neuromuscular blockade after surgery (neostigmine with glycopyrrolate); postoperative paralytic ileus and urinary retention (neostigmine); glaucoma (physostigmine, echothiophate — rarely now); belladonna/atropine and anticholinergic poisoning (physostigmine); Alzheimer disease (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine); cobra envenomation (neostigmine with atropine, post-synaptic neurotoxin); squint (accommodative esotropia — echothiophate). Adverse effects: muscarinic (salivation, sweating, bradycardia, bronchospasm, diarrhoea, miosis), nicotinic (fasciculations, weakness), cholinergic crisis.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Choline esters","ACh, methacholine, carbachol, bethanechol"],["Alkaloids","Pilocarpine, muscarine, arecoline"],["Reversible anti-ChE","Physostigmine, neostigmine, pyridostigmine, edrophonium, donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine"],["Irreversible anti-ChE","Organophosphates (malathion, parathion, sarin), carbamates (carbaryl)"]]],
["Uses of anticholinesterases",[["Use","Drug"],["Myasthenia gravis (diagnosis)","Edrophonium"],["Myasthenia gravis (treatment)","Pyridostigmine, neostigmine"],["Reversal of NM block","Neostigmine + glycopyrrolate"],["Atropine poisoning","Physostigmine"],["Postoperative ileus/retention","Neostigmine"],["Alzheimer's disease","Donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine"],["Glaucoma","Physostigmine, echothiophate (rare)"],["Cobra bite","Neostigmine"]]],
["Physostigmine vs neostigmine",[["Feature","Physostigmine","Neostigmine"],["Structure","Tertiary amine","Quaternary ammonium"],["CNS entry","Yes","No"],["Oral absorption","Good","Poor"],["Direct NM action","No","Yes"],["Main use","Atropine poisoning, glaucoma","Myasthenia, NM block reversal"]]]],
{c:"Anticholinesterases enhance cholinergic transmission; their selection depends on CNS penetration, duration and site of action needed."});

Z("pharm2",1,"ph",["Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease where antibodies destroy acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction, causing muscle weakness that worsens with use; it is treated with anticholinesterases and immunosuppression.","The 'locks' for acetylcholine on muscle are reduced, so the signal fades with repeated use; anticholinesterases keep more acetylcholine available to use the remaining locks.",["Anti-AChR antibodies (also anti-MuSK); thymoma/thymic hyperplasia associated.","Ptosis, diplopia, bulbar weakness, fatigable limb weakness; myasthenic crisis (respiratory failure).","Diagnosis: edrophonium/ice-pack test, antibodies, repetitive nerve stimulation (decrement), single-fibre EMG.","Treatment: pyridostigmine (mainstay), steroids, azathioprine, mycophenolate, thymectomy, plasmapheresis/IVIG for crisis; avoid aminoglycosides, quinine, β-blockers, magnesium."],"Myasthenia = 'Muscles tire with use; Pyridostigmine fixes'."],
"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder of neuromuscular transmission caused by antibodies against nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (85%) or muscle-specific kinase (MuSK) at the motor end-plate, reducing functional receptors through complement-mediated damage, receptor cross-linking and internalisation, and blockade. Associated with thymic hyperplasia and thymoma. Clinically: fluctuating, fatigable weakness of ocular (ptosis, diplopia), bulbar, facial, neck and limb muscles, worse in the evening and with exertion; myasthenic crisis with respiratory failure. Diagnosis: edrophonium (Tensilon) test — rapid transient improvement; ice-pack test for ptosis; anti-AChR and anti-MuSK antibodies; repetitive nerve stimulation showing decremental response; single-fibre EMG (jitter); CT of thorax for thymoma. Treatment: anticholinesterases — pyridostigmine (longer acting, fewer muscarinic side effects; 60 mg 4–6 hourly) or neostigmine, with atropine/glycopyrrolate for muscarinic side effects if needed; immunosuppression — prednisolone, azathioprine, mycophenolate, cyclosporine, tacrolimus, rituximab, eculizumab; thymectomy; plasmapheresis or IVIG for crisis and pre-operatively. Differentiation of myasthenic crisis (undertreatment) from cholinergic crisis (overdose of anticholinesterases — weakness with muscarinic signs) using edrophonium test (improves myasthenic, worsens cholinergic). Drugs that aggravate MG: aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, macrolides, quinine, β-blockers, magnesium, neuromuscular blockers, procainamide.",
[["Treatment",[["Approach","Drugs"],["Symptomatic","Pyridostigmine, neostigmine"],["Immunosuppression","Prednisolone, azathioprine, mycophenolate, rituximab"],["Surgery","Thymectomy"],["Crisis","Plasmapheresis, IVIG, ventilation"]]],
["Myasthenic vs cholinergic crisis",[["Feature","Myasthenic","Cholinergic"],["Cause","Undertreatment","Excess anticholinesterase"],["Muscarinic signs","Absent","Present (salivation, miosis, bradycardia)"],["Edrophonium","Improves","Worsens"]]]],
{c:"Myasthenia gravis is managed with anticholinesterases for symptoms and immunotherapy or thymectomy for the disease process; aggravating drugs must be avoided."});

Z("pharm2",2,"ph",["Anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) drugs block muscarinic acetylcholine receptors; atropine is the prototype, used for bradycardia, organophosphate poisoning, as pre-anaesthetic and for eye examination.","Atropine blocks the 'rest and digest' brakes, so the heart speeds up, secretions dry, pupils dilate and gut slows — 'dry as a bone, red as a beet, blind as a bat, hot as a hare, mad as a hatter'.",["Natural: atropine, hyoscine (scopolamine); semisynthetic/synthetic: homatropine, tropicamide, cyclopentolate (eye); ipratropium, tiotropium (asthma/COPD); glycopyrrolate (pre-anaesthetic); dicyclomine, hyoscine butylbromide (spasm); oxybutynin, tolterodine (bladder); benzhexol, benztropine (Parkinsonism).","Atropine actions: tachycardia (low dose bradycardia), ↓ secretions, mydriasis + cycloplegia, bronchodilation, ↓ GI/urinary motility, CNS excitation.","Uses: OP poisoning (large doses), sinus bradycardia/heart block, pre-anaesthetic, refraction testing, antispasmodic, motion sickness (hyoscine), iridocyclitis.","ADR/poisoning: dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention, constipation, hyperthermia, delirium; contraindicated in angle-closure glaucoma, BPH; antidote physostigmine."],"Atropine toxicity: 'Dry, Red, Blind, Hot, Mad'."],
"Anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) drugs competitively block muscarinic receptors. Classification: (1) natural alkaloids — atropine (from Atropa belladonna), hyoscine/scopolamine (Hyoscyamus); (2) semisynthetic derivatives — homatropine, hyoscine butylbromide, ipratropium bromide, tiotropium; (3) synthetic — mydriatics (cyclopentolate, tropicamide), antisecretory–antispasmodics (glycopyrrolate, propantheline, dicyclomine, oxyphenonium, pirenzepine — M1 selective), vesicoselective (oxybutynin, tolterodine, solifenacin, darifenacin, trospium), antiparkinsonian (trihexyphenidyl/benzhexol, benztropine, procyclidine), bronchodilators (ipratropium, tiotropium). Atropine pharmacological actions: CNS — mild stimulation, antiparkinsonian, high doses cause excitement, hallucinations, delirium (hyoscine produces sedation and amnesia, antimotion sickness); CVS — tachycardia (initial bradycardia with low doses due to central vagal stimulation or presynaptic M1 block), shortens AV conduction, flushing (atropine flush); eye — mydriasis, cycloplegia, loss of light reflex, raised intraocular pressure in narrow angle; secretions — decreased salivary, sweat (hyperthermia), bronchial, gastric secretions; smooth muscle — relaxation of GI, biliary, urinary tract (retention), bronchodilatation. Pharmacokinetics: well absorbed, crosses BBB and placenta, partly metabolised in liver, t½ about 4 h. Uses: antisecretory and pre-anaesthetic medication (glycopyrrolate preferred — no CNS effects), organophosphate and carbamate poisoning and mushroom (muscarinic) poisoning (atropine in large doses until atropinisation), sinus bradycardia and AV block (vagal), as mydriatic–cycloplegic for refraction in children and treatment of iritis/iridocyclitis (alternated with miotics to prevent synechiae), antispasmodic (intestinal and renal colic, dysmenorrhoea), urinary disorders (overactive bladder — oxybutynin, tolterodine; enuresis), bronchial asthma and COPD (ipratropium, tiotropium), motion sickness (hyoscine transdermal), Parkinsonism and drug-induced extrapyramidal symptoms (benzhexol), reversal of neostigmine muscarinic effects, carotid sinus hypersensitivity. Adverse effects: dry mouth, difficulty swallowing, blurred vision, photophobia, palpitation, constipation, urinary retention (BPH), hyperthermia (children), confusion and delirium in elderly. Atropine poisoning (belladonna, Datura): dry hot flushed skin, dilated fixed pupils, tachycardia, hyperpyrexia, delirium, hallucinations, urinary retention — 'mad as a hatter, red as a beet, dry as a bone, hot as a hare, blind as a bat'; treatment — gastric lavage, physostigmine (crosses BBB), diazepam for convulsions, cooling. Contraindications: angle-closure glaucoma, BPH, paralytic ileus, tachyarrhythmias.",
[["Classification",[["Category","Drugs"],["Natural","Atropine, hyoscine"],["Mydriatics","Homatropine, cyclopentolate, tropicamide"],["Antisecretory/antispasmodic","Glycopyrrolate, dicyclomine, hyoscine butylbromide, pirenzepine"],["Bronchodilators","Ipratropium, tiotropium"],["Bladder","Oxybutynin, tolterodine, solifenacin"],["Antiparkinsonian","Benzhexol, benztropine"]]],
["Atropine actions",[["System","Action"],["Heart","Tachycardia, ↑ AV conduction"],["Eye","Mydriasis, cycloplegia, ↑ IOP"],["Glands","↓ Salivary, sweat, bronchial secretions"],["GI/urinary","↓ Motility, retention"],["Bronchi","Bronchodilatation"],["CNS","Excitation (high dose)"]]],
["Uses",["Organophosphate poisoning","Pre-anaesthetic (glycopyrrolate)","Sinus bradycardia, heart block","Refraction, iridocyclitis","Colic, overactive bladder","Asthma/COPD (ipratropium)","Motion sickness (hyoscine)","Parkinsonism (benzhexol)"]]],
{i:"Atropine is a competitive antagonist at all muscarinic receptor subtypes; the sensitivity of organs differs — salivary and sweat glands most, gastric acid secretion least.",x:[["Atropine poisoning and treatment",["Features: dry mouth, hot flushed skin, hyperpyrexia, dilated pupils, tachycardia, delirium, urinary retention","Treatment: gastric lavage, physostigmine 1–2 mg IV, diazepam, cooling, catheterisation"]]],f:"Atropine → blocks muscarinic receptors\n ├ Heart: tachycardia\n ├ Eye: mydriasis, cycloplegia\n ├ Glands: dryness\n ├ GI/bladder: relaxation, retention\n └ CNS: excitation, delirium (high dose)",c:"Atropine and its congeners are versatile drugs; selecting agents with peripheral or organ selectivity minimises adverse effects."});

Z("pharm2",3,"ph",["Organophosphate poisoning (insecticides) irreversibly blocks acetylcholinesterase, flooding the body with acetylcholine; treat with atropine for muscarinic effects and pralidoxime to reactivate the enzyme early.","Like a flooded engine: acetylcholine everywhere causes secretions, small pupils and muscle twitching; atropine blocks the muscarinic flood, oximes repair the enzyme before it 'ages'.",["Muscarinic (DUMBELS): diarrhoea, urination, miosis, bronchorrhoea/bronchospasm, bradycardia, emesis, lacrimation, salivation/sweating.","Nicotinic: fasciculations, weakness, paralysis, tachycardia, hypertension; CNS: confusion, convulsions, coma.","Treatment: decontaminate (remove clothes, wash skin), airway/oxygen, atropine 2 mg IV doubling every 5 min till atropinisation (clear chest, HR > 80), pralidoxime 30 mg/kg then infusion, diazepam for seizures.","Diagnosis: clinical + reduced RBC/plasma cholinesterase; intermediate syndrome (24–96 h), delayed neuropathy."],"OP signs 'DUMBELS'; antidotes 'Atropine + Pralidoxime'."],
"Organophosphate (OP) and carbamate insecticides (parathion, malathion, chlorpyrifos, dichlorvos, monocrotophos; carbaryl, propoxur) are common agents of suicidal and accidental poisoning in India; nerve agents (sarin, tabun) also act similarly. They inhibit acetylcholinesterase by phosphorylation (carbamates by carbamylation — spontaneously reversible within hours); the phosphorylated enzyme undergoes 'ageing' (loss of an alkyl group) within hours, after which oximes cannot reactivate it. Accumulated ACh overstimulates muscarinic, nicotinic and central receptors. Features: muscarinic — miosis, blurred vision, lacrimation, salivation, sweating, bronchorrhoea, bronchospasm, vomiting, abdominal cramps, diarrhoea, urinary incontinence, bradycardia, hypotension (DUMBELS/SLUDGE); nicotinic — muscle fasciculations, cramps, weakness, flaccid paralysis including respiratory muscles, tachycardia, hypertension, mydriasis occasionally; CNS — anxiety, confusion, ataxia, convulsions, coma, central respiratory depression. Death from respiratory failure. Late complications: intermediate syndrome (24–96 h: proximal, neck flexor, cranial and respiratory muscle weakness) and organophosphate-induced delayed polyneuropathy (2–3 weeks). Diagnosis: history, smell (kerosene/garlic), clinical picture; reduced red cell (true) acetylcholinesterase and plasma butyrylcholinesterase. Treatment: remove from exposure, decontaminate skin and remove clothing (protect staff), gastric lavage only if early; maintain airway, suction, oxygen, ventilation; atropine — 1.8–3 mg IV, doubling every 5 minutes until atropinisation (clear chest on auscultation, heart rate > 80/min, systolic BP > 80 mmHg, dry axillae, pupils no longer pinpoint), then infusion; oximes — pralidoxime 30 mg/kg IV bolus then 8 mg/kg/h (reactivate phosphorylated enzyme by nucleophilic attack, mainly effective at nicotinic sites, given early before ageing; not useful in carbamates); diazepam for convulsions; avoid succinylcholine (prolonged block); monitor for intermediate syndrome. Glycopyrrolate may supplement atropine for peripheral effects.",
[["Clinical features",[["Receptor","Features"],["Muscarinic","Miosis, salivation, lacrimation, sweating, bronchorrhoea, bronchospasm, bradycardia, vomiting, diarrhoea, urination"],["Nicotinic","Fasciculations, weakness, paralysis, tachycardia, hypertension"],["CNS","Anxiety, confusion, convulsions, coma, respiratory depression"]]],
["Treatment",["Decontamination; protect staff","Airway, suction, O2, ventilation","Atropine — double dose every 5 min to atropinisation","Pralidoxime early (before ageing)","Diazepam for seizures","Monitor for intermediate syndrome"]]],
{c:"OP poisoning is a medical emergency; aggressive atropinisation, early oxime therapy and ventilatory support are the cornerstones of survival."});

Z("pharm2",4,"ph",["Adrenergic drugs mimic sympathetic effects by stimulating α and β receptors directly or by releasing noradrenaline; amphetamine is an indirect CNS stimulant and dopamine is used in shock because of dose-dependent receptor effects.","Adrenergic drugs press the 'fight or flight' accelerator; each drug presses different pedals (α1, β1, β2, dopamine).",["Direct: adrenaline (α + β), noradrenaline (α + β1), isoprenaline (β), dopamine (D1, β1, α), dobutamine (β1), phenylephrine (α1), salbutamol (β2), clonidine (α2).","Indirect: amphetamine, tyramine (release NA); mixed: ephedrine.","Dopamine dose effects: low (2–5 µg/kg/min) D1 — renal vasodilation; medium (5–10) β1 — inotropy; high (> 10) α1 — vasoconstriction. Use: cardiogenic shock.","Amphetamine: CNS stimulant — ADHD, narcolepsy; abuse potential, tolerance, psychosis."],"Dopamine dose: 'Low = Kidney, Mid = Heart, High = Vessels'."],
"Adrenergic (sympathomimetic) drugs mimic the actions of the sympathetic nervous system. Classification by mechanism: directly acting — catecholamines (adrenaline, noradrenaline, isoprenaline, dopamine, dobutamine) and non-catecholamines (phenylephrine, methoxamine, salbutamol, terbutaline, salmeterol, formoterol, clonidine, xylometazoline); indirectly acting — release stored noradrenaline (amphetamine, methamphetamine, tyramine), or block reuptake (cocaine); mixed — ephedrine, pseudoephedrine, mephentermine. By therapeutic use: pressor agents (noradrenaline, phenylephrine, ephedrine, mephentermine, dopamine), cardiac stimulants (adrenaline, isoprenaline, dobutamine), bronchodilators (salbutamol, terbutaline, salmeterol), nasal decongestants (phenylephrine, oxymetazoline, xylometazoline, pseudoephedrine), CNS stimulants (amphetamine, methylphenidate, modafinil), anorectics (sibutramine, withdrawn), uterine relaxants (ritodrine, isoxsuprine), vasodilators. Uses: anaphylactic shock (adrenaline IM — drug of choice), cardiac arrest (adrenaline), hypotension and septic shock (noradrenaline), cardiogenic shock and heart failure (dobutamine, dopamine), bronchial asthma (salbutamol), nasal decongestion, local anaesthesia (adrenaline prolongs action), control of local bleeding, mydriasis (phenylephrine), premature labour (β2 agonists), ADHD and narcolepsy (methylphenidate, amphetamine), heart block (isoprenaline). Amphetamine is an indirectly acting sympathomimetic that releases noradrenaline and dopamine centrally and peripherally; it causes alertness, euphoria, reduced fatigue and appetite, increased physical performance; used in ADHD and narcolepsy; adverse — insomnia, anxiety, tachycardia, hypertension, tolerance, psychological dependence, amphetamine psychosis. Dopamine acts on D1 (renal and mesenteric vasodilatation) at low doses (1–2/2–5 µg/kg/min), on β1 (increased contractility and cardiac output) at moderate doses (5–10 µg/kg/min) and on α1 (vasoconstriction) at high doses (> 10 µg/kg/min); used as IV infusion in cardiogenic and septic shock, especially with oliguria (renal-dose concept now discredited), and in acute heart failure; does not cross BBB; adverse — tachyarrhythmias, nausea, extravasation necrosis (treated with phentolamine infiltration).",
[["Receptor selectivity",[["Drug","Receptors","Main use"],["Adrenaline","α1, α2, β1, β2","Anaphylaxis, cardiac arrest"],["Noradrenaline","α1, α2, β1","Septic shock"],["Isoprenaline","β1, β2","Heart block"],["Dopamine","D1, β1, α1 (dose)","Shock"],["Dobutamine","β1","Cardiogenic shock, HF"],["Phenylephrine","α1","Hypotension, decongestant, mydriasis"],["Salbutamol","β2","Asthma"],["Clonidine","α2","Hypertension"]]],
["Dopamine dose–response",[["Dose (µg/kg/min)","Receptor","Effect"],["1–5","D1","Renal, mesenteric vasodilatation"],["5–10","β1","↑ Contractility, cardiac output"],["> 10","α1","Vasoconstriction, ↑ BP"]]]],
{c:"Adrenergic drugs are chosen by receptor selectivity for the needed effect; dopamine's dose-dependent actions make it adaptable in shock, while amphetamine's central effects carry abuse potential."});

Z("pharm2",5,"ph",["α-blockers block α-adrenergic receptors, relaxing blood vessels and smooth muscle of prostate and bladder neck; used in BPH, hypertension and pheochromocytoma.","α-blockers loosen the squeeze of α-receptors on vessels and the prostate — opening pipes and the urine outflow.",["Non-selective: phenoxybenzamine (irreversible — pheochromocytoma), phentolamine (reversible — hypertensive crisis, extravasation).","α1-selective: prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin (hypertension, BPH); uroselective α1A: tamsulosin, silodosin, alfuzosin (BPH).","α2: yohimbine (aphrodisiac claim).","ADR: first-dose hypotension (give at bedtime), postural hypotension, dizziness, nasal stuffiness, reflex tachycardia (non-selective), retrograde ejaculation (tamsulosin), floppy iris syndrome."],"α1A selective 'Tamsulosin = Tiny prostate effects, no BP fall'."],
"α-adrenergic blocking drugs antagonise the actions of catecholamines at α receptors. Classification: (1) non-selective (α1 + α2): irreversible — phenoxybenzamine (forms covalent bonds; long-acting); reversible — phentolamine, tolazoline; ergot alkaloids (ergotamine, dihydroergotamine — partial agonists); chlorpromazine (incidental); (2) α1-selective: prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin, alfuzosin; uroselective α1A: tamsulosin, silodosin; (3) α2-selective: yohimbine. Pharmacological effects: blockade of α1 receptors on arterioles and veins → vasodilatation, fall in BP (more on standing — postural hypotension), reflex tachycardia (more with non-selective agents as α2 blockade increases noradrenaline release); relaxation of smooth muscle of prostate, prostatic capsule and bladder neck (α1A) improving urine flow; nasal stuffiness; miosis; inhibition of ejaculation. Uses: benign prostatic hyperplasia (tamsulosin, silodosin, alfuzosin, terazosin, doxazosin — symptomatic relief of obstruction); hypertension (prazosin, doxazosin, terazosin — not first-line; useful with BPH; favourable lipid effects); pheochromocytoma — preoperative preparation with phenoxybenzamine (followed by β-blocker), intraoperative and hypertensive crises with phentolamine; hypertensive crisis due to clonidine withdrawal, cheese reaction (MAOI + tyramine) — phentolamine; local extravasation of noradrenaline/dopamine — phentolamine infiltration; Raynaud phenomenon and peripheral vascular disease (prazosin); scorpion sting (prazosin — India); ureteric stones (medical expulsive therapy — tamsulosin); erectile dysfunction (intracavernosal phentolamine with papaverine). Adverse effects: postural hypotension, first-dose effect (marked hypotension and syncope after the first dose of prazosin — start low at bedtime), dizziness, palpitation, nasal congestion, headache, fatigue, impotence/retrograde ejaculation, intraoperative floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery (tamsulosin), fluid retention.",
[["Classification",[["Type","Drugs","Uses"],["Non-selective irreversible","Phenoxybenzamine","Pheochromocytoma (preoperative)"],["Non-selective reversible","Phentolamine","Hypertensive crisis, extravasation"],["α1-selective","Prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin","Hypertension, BPH, scorpion sting, Raynaud"],["α1A uroselective","Tamsulosin, silodosin","BPH, ureteric stones"],["α2-selective","Yohimbine","—"]]],
["Adverse effects",["First-dose hypotension","Postural hypotension","Reflex tachycardia","Nasal stuffiness","Retrograde ejaculation","Floppy iris syndrome (tamsulosin)"]]],
{c:"α-blockers are mainly used for BPH and pheochromocytoma; uroselective agents relieve urinary obstruction with minimal effect on blood pressure."});

Z("pharm2",6,"ph",["β-blockers block β-adrenergic receptors, slowing the heart, reducing its force and lowering blood pressure; used in hypertension, angina, post-MI, heart failure, arrhythmias and many other conditions.","β-blockers press a brake on the heart's 'stress accelerator', making it beat slower and work less.",["Non-selective (β1 + β2): propranolol, timolol, nadolol, sotalol; β1-selective (cardioselective): metoprolol, atenolol, bisoprolol, esmolol, nebivolol; with α-block: labetalol, carvedilol; with ISA: pindolol.","Uses: hypertension, angina, post-MI, heart failure (metoprolol, bisoprolol, carvedilol), arrhythmias, thyrotoxicosis, migraine prophylaxis, essential tremor, anxiety, glaucoma (timolol), portal hypertension, HOCM, pheochromocytoma (after α-block).","ADR: bradycardia, heart block, bronchospasm, cold extremities, fatigue, masked hypoglycaemia, worsening of acute HF, sexual dysfunction, rebound on abrupt withdrawal, dyslipidaemia.","Contraindications: asthma/COPD (non-selective), heart block, acute decompensated HF, Prinzmetal angina, peripheral vascular disease (caution)."],"β-blocker selectivity 'A-M first (β1)': Atenolol, Metoprolol, Esmolol, Bisoprolol — cardioselective."],
"β-adrenergic blockers competitively antagonise catecholamines at β receptors. Classification: non-selective (β1 + β2) — propranolol (prototype; membrane stabilising, lipophilic), sotalol (also class III), timolol, nadolol (long-acting), pindolol (intrinsic sympathomimetic activity, ISA); β1-selective (cardioselective) — metoprolol, atenolol, acebutolol (ISA), bisoprolol, esmolol (ultra-short acting, IV), betaxolol, nebivolol (also NO-mediated vasodilatation); β with additional α1 blockade — labetalol, carvedilol. Pharmacological actions: heart — decreased rate, force, cardiac output, AV conduction, automaticity, myocardial oxygen demand; BP — gradual fall in hypertensives (reduced cardiac output, renin release, central sympathetic outflow, resetting baroreceptors); bronchi — bronchoconstriction (β2 block); metabolic — impaired glycogenolysis, masking of hypoglycaemic warning signs (tachycardia, tremor), increased triglycerides, decreased HDL; eye — reduced aqueous secretion (lower IOP); skeletal muscle — reduced tremor; CNS — anxiolysis (peripheral symptom control). Uses: hypertension; angina pectoris (not vasospastic); myocardial infarction (secondary prevention reduces mortality; early IV to limit infarct size); heart failure (stable chronic HFrEF — metoprolol succinate, bisoprolol, carvedilol, nebivolol — reduce mortality); arrhythmias (supraventricular tachycardia, AF rate control, digitalis-induced, perioperative — esmolol); hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy; dissecting aortic aneurysm; pheochromocytoma (after α-blockade); thyrotoxicosis (symptomatic, thyroid storm — propranolol also inhibits T4 → T3); migraine prophylaxis; essential tremor; anxiety (performance anxiety); glaucoma (timolol, betaxolol eye drops); portal hypertension (propranolol, carvedilol — prevent variceal bleeding); alcohol withdrawal; infantile haemangioma (propranolol). Adverse effects: bradycardia, AV block, precipitation of acute heart failure, bronchospasm in asthmatics (even topical timolol), cold extremities and worsening of peripheral vascular disease and Raynaud's, fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, sleep disturbances and nightmares (lipophilic agents), depression, sexual dysfunction, masked hypoglycaemia and delayed recovery in diabetics on insulin, dyslipidaemia, rebound hypertension/angina/MI on abrupt withdrawal (taper), interaction with verapamil/diltiazem (heart block). Contraindications: bronchial asthma and COPD (non-selective; cardioselective cautiously), sinus bradycardia, second/third-degree heart block, acute decompensated heart failure, cardiogenic shock, Prinzmetal (vasospastic) angina, severe peripheral vascular disease, uncontrolled diabetes (relative).",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs","Features"],["Non-selective","Propranolol, timolol, nadolol, sotalol","β1 + β2 block"],["With ISA","Pindolol, acebutolol","Less bradycardia"],["Cardioselective","Metoprolol, atenolol, bisoprolol, esmolol, nebivolol","β1 > β2"],["With α-block","Labetalol, carvedilol","Vasodilatation"]]],
["Uses",[["Use","Preferred agent"],["Hypertension","Metoprolol, atenolol, nebivolol"],["Angina, post-MI","Metoprolol, atenolol, propranolol"],["Heart failure","Bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol succinate"],["Arrhythmias","Esmolol, propranolol, sotalol"],["Thyrotoxicosis","Propranolol"],["Migraine prophylaxis, tremor","Propranolol"],["Glaucoma","Timolol"],["Portal hypertension","Propranolol, carvedilol"],["Hypertension in pregnancy","Labetalol"]]],
["Adverse effects",["Bradycardia, heart block","Bronchospasm","Cold extremities","Fatigue, nightmares","Masked hypoglycaemia","Rebound on sudden withdrawal","Dyslipidaemia, sexual dysfunction"]]],
{i:"β-blockers are among the most widely used cardiovascular drugs; cardioselectivity, ISA and α-blocking properties tailor them to different patients.",f:"β-blocker → β1 block (heart): ↓ HR, ↓ contractility, ↓ renin → ↓ BP, ↓ O2 demand\n → β2 block: bronchospasm, ↓ glycogenolysis, cold extremities\nUses: HTN, angina, MI, HF, arrhythmia, thyrotoxicosis, migraine, glaucoma, varices",c:"β-blockers have wide cardiovascular and non-cardiovascular uses; choosing the right subclass and avoiding them in asthma, heart block and acute heart failure maximise safety."});

Z("pharm2",7,"ph",["Glaucoma is raised eye pressure that damages the optic nerve. Open-angle glaucoma is chronic and treated with eye drops that reduce aqueous production or increase outflow; angle-closure glaucoma is an emergency treated with rapid IOP lowering then laser iridotomy.","The eye is a ball with a tap (ciliary body making fluid) and a drain (trabecular meshwork). Drugs either turn down the tap or open the drain; in angle-closure the drain is suddenly blocked by the iris.",["Increase outflow: prostaglandin analogues (latanoprost — uveoscleral, first-line), miotics (pilocarpine — trabecular, angle-closure), rho-kinase inhibitors (netarsudil).","Decrease production: β-blockers (timolol), α2 agonists (brimonidine), carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (dorzolamide topical, acetazolamide oral/IV).","Osmotic agents for acute attack: mannitol IV, glycerol oral.","Angle-closure emergency: IV acetazolamide + mannitol + topical timolol, pilocarpine (after IOP falls), steroids; then laser peripheral iridotomy. Avoid atropine/mydriatics."],"Acute angle closure: 'Acetazolamide, Mannitol, Timolol, Pilocarpine → Laser'."],
"Glaucoma is a group of optic neuropathies with characteristic cupping of the optic disc and visual field loss, usually associated with raised intraocular pressure (> 21 mmHg). Aqueous humour is secreted by the ciliary epithelium (stimulated by β2 receptors and carbonic anhydrase) and drains mainly through the trabecular meshwork and canal of Schlemm (conventional, increased by ciliary muscle contraction) and the uveoscleral pathway. Drugs lower IOP by reducing aqueous formation or increasing outflow. Classes: (1) prostaglandin analogues — latanoprost, travoprost, bimatoprost, tafluprost: increase uveoscleral outflow; once-daily at night; first-line in open-angle glaucoma; adverse — iris and periocular pigmentation, eyelash growth, conjunctival hyperaemia, CME; (2) β-blockers — timolol, betaxolol (β1, safer in asthma), levobunolol, carteolol: reduce aqueous secretion; systemic absorption may cause bradycardia and bronchospasm (punctal occlusion reduces); (3) α2 agonists — brimonidine, apraclonidine: reduce secretion and increase uveoscleral outflow; adverse — allergy, dry mouth, CNS depression in infants; (4) carbonic anhydrase inhibitors — topical dorzolamide, brinzolamide; systemic acetazolamide (oral/IV), methazolamide: reduce bicarbonate and aqueous production; systemic adverse effects — paraesthesia, metabolic acidosis, hypokalaemia, renal stones, sulphonamide allergy; (5) miotics (cholinergic) — pilocarpine, carbachol: ciliary muscle contraction opens trabecular meshwork; miosis pulls iris away from angle (used in angle closure); adverse — brow ache, accommodative spasm, dim vision, retinal detachment; (6) rho kinase inhibitor — netarsudil; (7) osmotic agents — IV mannitol 20%, oral glycerol: dehydrate vitreous rapidly in acute attacks; (8) sympathomimetics (dipivefrine — rarely). Open-angle glaucoma (chronic, painless, progressive field loss): start with a prostaglandin analogue or timolol; add second agent (brimonidine, dorzolamide, fixed combinations) if target IOP not reached; laser trabeculoplasty or trabeculectomy if medical therapy fails. Acute angle-closure glaucoma (sudden painful red eye, haloes, blurred vision, nausea, fixed semi-dilated pupil, very high IOP; precipitated by mydriatics, dim light, anticholinergics): ophthalmic emergency — IV acetazolamide 500 mg, IV mannitol 1–2 g/kg (or oral glycerol), topical timolol, apraclonidine/brimonidine, topical steroids, analgesics and antiemetics; pilocarpine 1–2% once IOP has begun to fall (ischaemic iris sphincter unresponsive at very high IOP); definitive treatment — laser peripheral iridotomy in affected eye and prophylactically in fellow eye; avoid atropine and other mydriatics. Drugs that can precipitate angle closure: anticholinergics, sympathomimetics, antihistamines, tricyclic antidepressants, topiramate.",
[["Drug classes",[["Class","Drugs","Mechanism"],["Prostaglandin analogues","Latanoprost, bimatoprost","↑ Uveoscleral outflow"],["β-blockers","Timolol, betaxolol","↓ Aqueous production"],["α2 agonists","Brimonidine, apraclonidine","↓ Production, ↑ uveoscleral outflow"],["CA inhibitors","Dorzolamide, acetazolamide","↓ Production"],["Miotics","Pilocarpine","↑ Trabecular outflow"],["Rho kinase inhibitor","Netarsudil","↑ Trabecular outflow"],["Osmotics","Mannitol, glycerol","Vitreous dehydration"]]],
["Open-angle vs angle-closure treatment",[["Feature","Open-angle","Angle-closure (acute)"],["Onset","Chronic, painless","Acute, painful"],["First drugs","Latanoprost or timolol","IV acetazolamide + mannitol + timolol"],["Miotics","Rarely","Pilocarpine after IOP falls"],["Definitive","Laser trabeculoplasty/trabeculectomy","Laser peripheral iridotomy"],["Avoid","—","Mydriatics, anticholinergics"]]]],
{i:"Glaucoma is a leading cause of irreversible blindness; lowering IOP is the only proven way to slow optic nerve damage.",f:"Ciliary body → aqueous (β2, CA) → trabecular meshwork / uveoscleral → out\n↓ Production: timolol, brimonidine, dorzolamide/acetazolamide\n↑ Outflow: latanoprost (uveoscleral), pilocarpine (trabecular)\nAcute ACG: acetazolamide + mannitol + timolol → pilocarpine → iridotomy",c:"Open-angle glaucoma is treated with long-term topical drugs (prostaglandin analogues first-line), while acute angle closure needs urgent systemic and topical IOP lowering followed by laser iridotomy."});

Z("pharm3",0,"ph",["H1 antihistamines block histamine H1 receptors and relieve allergic symptoms; first-generation drugs cause sedation, second-generation drugs are non-sedating.","Antihistamines put a cover over the histamine 'alarm bell' (H1 receptor), stopping itching, sneezing and swelling.",["First-generation (sedating): diphenhydramine, promethazine, chlorpheniramine, hydroxyzine, cyproheptadine, dimenhydrinate, meclizine.","Second-generation (non-sedating): cetirizine, levocetirizine, loratadine, desloratadine, fexofenadine, bilastine.","Uses: allergic rhinitis, urticaria, pruritus, motion sickness (promethazine, meclizine, dimenhydrinate), vertigo, nausea of pregnancy (doxylamine), sedation, anaphylaxis (adjunct), common cold, drug-induced extrapyramidal reactions (promethazine).","ADR: sedation, dizziness, anticholinergic effects (dry mouth, retention, blurred vision), impaired psychomotor function; toxicity — CNS excitation and convulsions in children, QT (terfenadine/astemizole — withdrawn)."],"1st gen = 'sleepy + anticholinergic'; 2nd gen = 'awake'."],
"H1 receptor antagonists (antihistamines) are inverse agonists at H1 receptors, competitively blocking histamine actions on smooth muscle, vascular permeability and nerve endings. Classification: first-generation (cross the BBB, sedating, anticholinergic) — highly sedating: diphenhydramine, dimenhydrinate, promethazine, hydroxyzine; moderately sedating: pheniramine, cyproheptadine, meclizine, cinnarizine; mildly sedating: chlorpheniramine, dexchlorpheniramine, triprolidine, clemastine; second-generation (non-sedating, more H1-selective, little anticholinergic action, longer acting) — fexofenadine, loratadine, desloratadine, cetirizine, levocetirizine, azelastine (topical), mizolastine, ebastine, rupatadine, bilastine. Pharmacological actions: antagonism of histamine-induced bronchoconstriction (limited clinical value in asthma), vasodilatation and increased capillary permeability (reduced wheal, flare, itch), CNS depression (sedation) or stimulation (in children and overdose), anticholinergic and antiemetic effects (first-generation), local anaesthetic (diphenhydramine, promethazine), and anti-5-HT (cyproheptadine). Uses: allergic disorders — seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis, allergic conjunctivitis, urticaria, angioedema, atopic dermatitis and pruritus, insect bites, mild transfusion reactions, drug allergies; anaphylaxis (adjunct after adrenaline); motion sickness (promethazine, dimenhydrinate, meclizine, cinnarizine taken 30–60 minutes before journey); vertigo and Ménière disease (cinnarizine, dimenhydrinate); morning sickness (doxylamine with pyridoxine); preanaesthetic sedation and as hypnotic (promethazine, diphenhydramine — short term); antiemetic; cough (in combinations); parkinsonism and drug-induced extrapyramidal reactions (diphenhydramine, promethazine — anticholinergic); appetite stimulation (cyproheptadine); common cold (dries secretions). Adverse effects: sedation, drowsiness, impaired psychomotor performance (driving hazard), dizziness, fatigue, potentiation of alcohol and CNS depressants, paradoxical excitement in children, anticholinergic effects (dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention, constipation — caution in BPH, glaucoma), GI upset, increased appetite and weight gain (cyproheptadine, ketotifen), allergic dermatitis with topical use; cardiac arrhythmias (QT prolongation, torsades) with terfenadine and astemizole when combined with CYP3A4 inhibitors (withdrawn). Toxicity/overdose: CNS excitation, hallucinations, ataxia, convulsions (especially in children), fever, dilated pupils, coma — resembles atropine poisoning; treatment supportive, diazepam for seizures.",
[["First vs second generation",[["Feature","First generation","Second generation"],["Examples","Chlorpheniramine, promethazine, diphenhydramine","Cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine"],["BBB penetration","Yes","Minimal"],["Sedation","Marked","Little/none"],["Anticholinergic","Present","Absent"],["Antiemetic, antimotion sickness","Yes","No"],["Duration","Short (4–6 h)","Long (12–24 h)"]]],
["Uses",["Allergic rhinitis, urticaria, pruritus","Motion sickness, vertigo","Nausea of pregnancy (doxylamine)","Sedation, preanaesthetic","Drug-induced extrapyramidal symptoms","Adjunct in anaphylaxis"]]],
{c:"Second-generation antihistamines are preferred for allergies because they avoid sedation, while first-generation agents remain useful for motion sickness, nausea and sedation."});

Z("pharm3",1,"ph",["Migraine is treated in two ways: drugs that abort an attack (NSAIDs, triptans, ergots) and drugs taken daily to prevent attacks (propranolol, amitriptyline, flunarizine, topiramate, valproate).","Triptans are targeted plumbers: they tighten swollen brain vessels and block pain-signal release from the trigeminal nerve.",["Mild attacks: paracetamol, NSAIDs (aspirin, naproxen) + antiemetic (metoclopramide, domperidone).","Moderate–severe: triptans (sumatriptan — 5-HT1B/1D agonist), ergotamine (rarely now); newer — gepants (CGRP antagonists), lasmiditan (5-HT1F).","Sumatriptan: 5-HT1B (vasoconstriction of cranial vessels) + 5-HT1D (inhibits CGRP release); oral, SC, nasal; ADR — chest tightness, tingling; contraindicated in IHD, uncontrolled hypertension, with ergots/MAOIs.","Prophylaxis (≥ 4 attacks/month): propranolol, amitriptyline, flunarizine, topiramate, valproate, CGRP monoclonal antibodies (erenumab)."],"Triptan = 'B constricts vessels, D blocks CGRP'."],
"Migraine is a primary headache disorder with recurrent unilateral pulsating headaches, nausea, photophobia and phonophobia, with or without aura, involving activation of the trigeminovascular system, release of vasoactive neuropeptides (CGRP, substance P), neurogenic inflammation and cranial vasodilatation, and cortical spreading depression in aura. Treatment of acute attack: mild to moderate — simple analgesics (paracetamol, aspirin 600–900 mg, ibuprofen, naproxen, diclofenac) taken early, with antiemetics/prokinetics (metoclopramide, domperidone) that also improve absorption; moderate to severe or unresponsive — triptans (sumatriptan, rizatriptan, zolmitriptan, naratriptan, eletriptan), ergot alkaloids (ergotamine, dihydroergotamine — now less used due to adverse effects), newer agents — gepants (ubrogepant, rimegepant — CGRP receptor antagonists) and ditans (lasmiditan — 5-HT1F agonist without vasoconstriction); status migrainosus — IV fluids, metoclopramide/prochlorperazine, dihydroergotamine, dexamethasone. Sumatriptan: selective 5-HT1B/1D agonist; 5-HT1B activation constricts dilated cranial extracerebral vessels; 5-HT1D activation inhibits release of CGRP and other inflammatory neuropeptides from trigeminal nerve endings and reduces pain transmission in trigeminal nucleus. Given orally (50–100 mg; bioavailability 15%), subcutaneously (6 mg; fastest), intranasally; t½ 2 h; headache recurrence common. Adverse effects: tingling, warmth, chest tightness or pressure, dizziness, coronary vasospasm (rare MI), serotonin syndrome with SSRIs/MAOIs. Contraindications: ischaemic heart disease, Prinzmetal angina, uncontrolled hypertension, cerebrovascular and peripheral vascular disease, pregnancy (relative), use within 24 h of ergotamine, hemiplegic/basilar migraine. Prophylaxis (indicated for frequent — ≥ 4 days/month — or disabling attacks): β-blockers (propranolol, metoprolol), tricyclic antidepressants (amitriptyline), calcium channel blockers (flunarizine), antiepileptics (topiramate, sodium valproate), anti-CGRP monoclonal antibodies (erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab), onabotulinumtoxin A (chronic migraine), candesartan; plus lifestyle and trigger avoidance. Medication overuse headache should be avoided by limiting acute drugs to < 10 days/month.",
[["Acute treatment",[["Severity","Drugs"],["Mild–moderate","Paracetamol, aspirin, NSAIDs + metoclopramide/domperidone"],["Moderate–severe","Triptans (sumatriptan), ergotamine"],["Newer","Gepants (CGRP antagonists), lasmiditan"]]],
["Prophylaxis",[["Class","Drug"],["β-blockers","Propranolol"],["TCAs","Amitriptyline"],["Calcium channel blocker","Flunarizine"],["Antiepileptics","Topiramate, valproate"],["Anti-CGRP mAb","Erenumab"],["Others","Onabotulinumtoxin A, candesartan"]]]],
{c:"Migraine treatment combines early acute therapy with triptans or NSAIDs and preventive drugs for frequent attacks; triptans must be avoided in coronary disease."});

Z("pharm3",2,"ph",["Prostaglandin analogues are synthetic versions of natural prostaglandins used for labour induction, abortion, glaucoma, peptic ulcer prevention, pulmonary hypertension and keeping the ductus arteriosus open.","Prostaglandins are local messengers; their analogues are used like precise tools to soften the cervix, open eye drains, protect the stomach or widen lung vessels.",["Misoprostol (PGE1): NSAID ulcer prevention, medical abortion (with mifepristone), PPH, cervical ripening.","Dinoprostone (PGE2): cervical ripening and labour induction; carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α): PPH, abortion.","Latanoprost, bimatoprost (PGF2α): glaucoma (↑ uveoscleral outflow).","Alprostadil (PGE1): keep ductus arteriosus patent in duct-dependent congenital heart disease, erectile dysfunction; epoprostenol, iloprost, treprostinil (PGI2): pulmonary arterial hypertension."],"PGE1 = misoprostol & alprostadil; PGE2 = dinoprostone; PGF2α = carboprost & latanoprost; PGI2 = epoprostenol."],
"Prostaglandins are eicosanoids synthesised from arachidonic acid by cyclooxygenase; their natural forms are rapidly metabolised, so stable synthetic analogues are used therapeutically. Uses: (1) obstetrics — termination of pregnancy: first-trimester medical abortion with mifepristone followed by misoprostol (PGE1) orally/vaginally/sublingually; second-trimester abortion with misoprostol, carboprost or dinoprostone; cervical priming before surgical abortion; induction of labour and cervical ripening at term — dinoprostone (PGE2) gel/pessary, misoprostol; postpartum haemorrhage (atonic uterus) — carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α) IM, misoprostol; (2) peptic ulcer — misoprostol prevents NSAID-induced gastric ulcers (inhibits acid secretion, increases mucus and bicarbonate, enhances mucosal blood flow); adverse — diarrhoea, abdominal cramps, contraindicated in pregnancy; (3) glaucoma — latanoprost, travoprost, bimatoprost, tafluprost (PGF2α analogues) increase uveoscleral outflow; first-line in open-angle glaucoma; bimatoprost also for eyelash hypotrichosis; (4) cardiovascular — alprostadil (PGE1) infusion to maintain patency of ductus arteriosus in neonates with duct-dependent congenital heart disease awaiting surgery; epoprostenol (PGI2, IV infusion), treprostinil, iloprost (inhaled) and selexipag (oral IP receptor agonist) for pulmonary arterial hypertension; epoprostenol to prevent platelet aggregation during dialysis/bypass; (5) erectile dysfunction — alprostadil intracavernosal or intraurethral; (6) others — peripheral vascular disease (iloprost), to close PDA indomethacin/ibuprofen (inhibit PG synthesis). Adverse effects: nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal cramps, fever, flushing, uterine hyperstimulation and rupture, bronchospasm (PGF2α — avoid carboprost in asthma), hypotension (PGE, PGI2), apnoea in neonates (alprostadil), iris pigmentation (latanoprost).",
[["Prostaglandin analogues and uses",[["Drug","Type","Uses"],["Misoprostol","PGE1","NSAID ulcer, abortion, PPH, labour induction"],["Alprostadil","PGE1","PDA patency, erectile dysfunction"],["Dinoprostone","PGE2","Cervical ripening, labour induction, abortion"],["Carboprost","15-methyl PGF2α","PPH, abortion"],["Latanoprost, bimatoprost","PGF2α","Glaucoma"],["Epoprostenol, iloprost, treprostinil","PGI2","Pulmonary arterial hypertension"]]]],
{c:"Prostaglandin analogues have targeted uses in obstetrics, ophthalmology, gastroenterology and cardiovascular medicine; their uterine stimulant effect contraindicates misoprostol in pregnancy except for intended abortion."});

Z("pharm3",3,"ph",["NSAIDs relieve pain, fever and inflammation by blocking cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes that make prostaglandins. Aspirin irreversibly blocks COX, and in low doses prevents platelet clotting (heart attacks, strokes).","Prostaglandins are the body's 'pain and fire alarm' signals; NSAIDs cut the wire to the alarm. Aspirin cuts it permanently in platelets, which can't make new wires.",["Classification: non-selective COX inhibitors (aspirin, ibuprofen, diclofenac, naproxen, indomethacin, piroxicam, ketorolac, mefenamic acid), preferential COX-2 (nimesulide, meloxicam, aceclofenac), selective COX-2 (celecoxib, etoricoxib), analgesic-antipyretic with poor anti-inflammatory action (paracetamol, metamizol).","Aspirin MOA: irreversible acetylation of COX-1/COX-2 → ↓ PGs (analgesic, antipyretic, anti-inflammatory) and ↓ TXA2 in platelets (antiplatelet for life of platelet, 7–10 days).","Aspirin uses: low dose (75–150 mg) — MI, stroke, unstable angina prevention; analgesic/antipyretic (300–600 mg); anti-inflammatory (3–5 g/day) — rheumatic fever; pre-eclampsia prevention.","ADR: gastric irritation, peptic ulcer, bleeding, Reye's syndrome in children, aspirin-induced asthma, salicylism (tinnitus, vertigo), hypersensitivity; toxicity — respiratory alkalosis then metabolic acidosis; treat with alkaline diuresis, haemodialysis."],"Aspirin dose: 'Low = platelets (75–150), Medium = pain/fever, High = inflammation'."],
"Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) are analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory agents that inhibit cyclooxygenase (prostaglandin synthase). COX-1 is constitutive (gastric mucosal protection, platelet TXA2, renal blood flow); COX-2 is induced at inflammatory sites (also constitutive in kidney, brain, endothelium — PGI2). Classification: (A) non-selective COX inhibitors — salicylates (aspirin), propionic acid derivatives (ibuprofen, naproxen, ketoprofen, flurbiprofen), fenamates (mefenamic acid), enolic acid derivatives (piroxicam, tenoxicam), acetic acid derivatives (ketorolac, indomethacin, nabumetone), pyrazolones (phenylbutazone, metamizol), aryl-acetic acids (diclofenac, aceclofenac); (B) preferential COX-2 inhibitors — nimesulide, diclofenac, aceclofenac, meloxicam, etodolac; (C) selective COX-2 inhibitors (coxibs) — celecoxib, etoricoxib, parecoxib; (D) analgesic–antipyretics with poor anti-inflammatory action — paracetamol (central COX inhibition), metamizol, propiphenazone, nefopam. Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid): mechanism — irreversible acetylation of a serine residue of COX-1 and COX-2, inhibiting prostaglandin and thromboxane synthesis; since platelets lack nuclei and cannot synthesise new COX, low doses suppress TXA2 for the platelet's lifespan (7–10 days), while endothelial cells regenerate COX and PGI2. Actions: analgesic (peripheral reduction of PG sensitisation of nociceptors; effective for inflammatory, musculoskeletal, dental pain, not visceral), antipyretic (reduces PGE2 in hypothalamus, resets thermostat; does not lower normal temperature), anti-inflammatory (high doses), antiplatelet, uricosuric at high doses (retention at low doses), respiratory stimulation then depression, metabolic effects (uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation), GI irritation. Pharmacokinetics: absorbed from stomach and upper intestine, hydrolysed to salicylate, protein bound, metabolised in liver (glycine and glucuronide conjugation), saturation kinetics at high doses (zero-order), excreted in urine (enhanced by alkalinisation). Uses: analgesic (headache, myalgia, arthralgia, dysmenorrhoea) 300–600 mg 6–8 hourly; antipyretic; acute rheumatic fever (4–6 g/day in divided doses); rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis (now replaced by other NSAIDs); antiplatelet — secondary prevention of MI, stroke and TIA, unstable angina, after CABG and stents (with P2Y12 inhibitor), atrial fibrillation (if anticoagulants contraindicated) at 75–150 mg/day; prevention of pre-eclampsia in high-risk women; Kawasaki disease; colorectal cancer prevention (Lynch syndrome); PDA closure (indomethacin preferred). Adverse effects: GI — epigastric distress, nausea, erosive gastritis, peptic ulcer, occult blood loss, haemorrhage; bleeding tendency; hypersensitivity — urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm (aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease — Samter triad with nasal polyps; due to shunting of arachidonic acid to leukotrienes); Reye syndrome in children with viral infections (avoid < 16 years); salicylism at high doses (tinnitus, deafness, vertigo, headache, confusion, sweating, hyperventilation); renal — sodium and water retention, reduced efficacy of antihypertensives and diuretics, analgesic nephropathy; hepatotoxicity; precipitation of gout at low doses; haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; prolonged labour and premature closure of ductus in late pregnancy. Acute salicylate poisoning: vomiting, tinnitus, hyperventilation with respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis, hyperthermia, dehydration, hypoglycaemia/hyperglycaemia, convulsions, coma, pulmonary oedema; treatment — gastric lavage, activated charcoal, IV fluids, sodium bicarbonate (alkaline diuresis to enhance excretion), correct hypoglycaemia and hypokalaemia, external cooling, vitamin K for bleeding, haemodialysis in severe cases. Contraindications: peptic ulcer, bleeding disorders, children with viral fevers, aspirin-sensitive asthma, late pregnancy, before surgery (stop 7 days), G6PD deficiency.",
[["Classification of NSAIDs",[["Group","Drugs"],["Non-selective","Aspirin, ibuprofen, naproxen, diclofenac, indomethacin, piroxicam, ketorolac, mefenamic acid"],["Preferential COX-2","Nimesulide, meloxicam, aceclofenac, etodolac"],["Selective COX-2","Celecoxib, etoricoxib, parecoxib"],["Analgesic-antipyretic","Paracetamol, metamizol"]]],
["Aspirin dose-dependent uses",[["Dose","Use"],["75–150 mg/day","Antiplatelet: MI, stroke prevention, ACS, pre-eclampsia"],["300–600 mg 6–8 hourly","Analgesic, antipyretic"],["3–5 g/day","Anti-inflammatory: acute rheumatic fever"]]],
["Adverse effects of aspirin",["Gastritis, peptic ulcer, GI bleeding","Bleeding tendency","Aspirin-induced asthma","Reye's syndrome in children","Salicylism (tinnitus, vertigo)","Hypersensitivity","Na⁺/water retention"]]],
{i:"NSAIDs are the most commonly used drugs worldwide; aspirin, the prototype, is unique in irreversibly inhibiting COX, giving it lasting antiplatelet action.",x:[["Salicylate poisoning management",["Activated charcoal, gastric lavage","IV fluids, correct K⁺ and glucose","Sodium bicarbonate — urinary alkalinisation","Cooling, vitamin K","Haemodialysis if severe"]],["COX-2 selective inhibitors","Fewer GI ulcers but increased risk of MI and stroke (↓ PGI2 with unopposed TXA2); rofecoxib withdrawn."]],f:"Arachidonic acid → COX-1/COX-2 → PGs, TXA2, PGI2\nAspirin → irreversible acetylation of COX\n ├ ↓ PGs → analgesia, antipyresis, anti-inflammation\n ├ ↓ platelet TXA2 → antiplatelet (7–10 days)\n └ ↓ gastric PG → ulcer; ↑ LTs → asthma",c:"NSAIDs relieve pain, fever and inflammation by COX inhibition; aspirin's irreversible action makes low-dose aspirin a cornerstone of cardiovascular prevention, while GI and bleeding risks limit higher doses."});

Z("pharm3",4,"ph",["DMARDs (disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs) slow the joint damage in rheumatoid arthritis, unlike NSAIDs which only relieve symptoms; methotrexate is the anchor drug.","NSAIDs turn down the volume of the alarm; DMARDs fix the faulty wiring that keeps triggering it.",["Conventional synthetic: methotrexate (first choice), sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine, leflunomide; older — gold, penicillamine, azathioprine, cyclosporine.","Biological: TNF-α inhibitors (etanercept, infliximab, adalimumab), IL-6 blocker (tocilizumab), B-cell depleter (rituximab), T-cell costimulation blocker (abatacept), IL-1 antagonist (anakinra).","Targeted synthetic: JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, baricitinib).","Slow onset (weeks–months); use with NSAIDs/low-dose steroids as bridge; monitor blood counts, LFT (MTX), eyes (HCQ), TB screening before biologics."],"DMARD anchor = Methotrexate weekly + folic acid."],
"Disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs) suppress the underlying immune-mediated process in rheumatoid arthritis and related diseases, retarding joint erosion and disease progression, unlike NSAIDs which provide symptomatic relief only. Their onset is slow (weeks to months). Classification: (1) conventional synthetic DMARDs (csDMARDs) — immunosuppressants: methotrexate (anchor drug; low-dose weekly; inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and increases adenosine — anti-inflammatory), leflunomide (pyrimidine synthesis inhibitor — DHODH), azathioprine, cyclosporine, cyclophosphamide; sulfasalazine; antimalarials — hydroxychloroquine, chloroquine; older — gold salts (auranofin, aurothiomalate), D-penicillamine, minocycline; (2) biological DMARDs (bDMARDs) — TNF-α inhibitors (etanercept — soluble receptor fusion protein; infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab, certolizumab — monoclonal antibodies), IL-1 receptor antagonist (anakinra), IL-6 receptor antibody (tocilizumab, sarilumab), T-cell costimulation modulator (abatacept — CTLA4-Ig), anti-CD20 B-cell depletion (rituximab); (3) targeted synthetic DMARDs (tsDMARDs) — Janus kinase inhibitors (tofacitinib, baricitinib, upadacitinib). Adjuncts: NSAIDs and low-dose glucocorticoids (bridge therapy, flares). Strategy: early aggressive treatment ('window of opportunity'), treat-to-target (remission or low disease activity), start methotrexate (7.5–25 mg once weekly with folic acid) as monotherapy or combination (methotrexate + sulfasalazine + hydroxychloroquine — triple therapy), add biologic or JAK inhibitor if inadequate response. Adverse effects and monitoring: methotrexate — mucositis, myelosuppression, hepatotoxicity/fibrosis, pneumonitis, teratogenic (CBC, LFT, creatinine); hydroxychloroquine — retinopathy (annual eye examination), GI upset; sulfasalazine — GI upset, rash, myelosuppression, oligospermia, haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; leflunomide — hepatotoxicity, diarrhoea, teratogenic; biologics — serious infections, reactivation of tuberculosis (screen with IGRA/Mantoux and chest X-ray), hepatitis B reactivation, infusion reactions, demyelination, lymphoma; JAK inhibitors — herpes zoster, thrombosis, cardiovascular events.",
[["Classification",[["Category","Drugs"],["Conventional synthetic","Methotrexate, sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine, leflunomide, azathioprine, cyclosporine"],["Biological — anti-TNF","Etanercept, infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab"],["Biological — others","Tocilizumab (IL-6R), rituximab (CD20), abatacept (CTLA4-Ig), anakinra (IL-1R)"],["Targeted synthetic","Tofacitinib, baricitinib (JAK)"],["Older","Gold salts, D-penicillamine"]]],
["Monitoring",[["Drug","Monitor"],["Methotrexate","CBC, LFT, renal function; folic acid supplement"],["Hydroxychloroquine","Retinal examination"],["Sulfasalazine","CBC, LFT; G6PD"],["Biologics","TB, HBV screening; infections"]]]],
{c:"DMARDs, led by methotrexate, alter the course of rheumatoid arthritis; early treat-to-target therapy with biologics or JAK inhibitors for resistant cases prevents joint destruction."});

Z("pharm3",5,"ph",["Gout is treated by stopping the acute attack (NSAIDs, colchicine, steroids) and by lowering uric acid long term (allopurinol, febuxostat, uricosurics).","For acute gout, put out the fire (inflammation); for chronic gout, reduce the fuel (uric acid).",["Acute gout: NSAIDs (indomethacin, naproxen), colchicine (low dose), corticosteroids; do not start/stop urate-lowering drugs during attack.","Colchicine: binds tubulin → inhibits neutrophil migration and phagocytosis of crystals; ADR — diarrhoea, vomiting, myopathy, marrow suppression.","Allopurinol: xanthine oxidase inhibitor (oxypurinol active metabolite); start low; ADR — rash, SJS/TEN (HLA-B*5801), precipitates acute gout initially (cover with colchicine); interaction ↑ azathioprine/6-MP toxicity.","Febuxostat (non-purine XO inhibitor); uricosurics — probenecid, lesinurad; rasburicase (tumour lysis); avoid aspirin, thiazides."],"Allopurinol + azathioprine = toxicity (reduce dose by 75%)."],
"Gout is a metabolic disorder of hyperuricaemia with deposition of monosodium urate crystals causing acute arthritis, tophi and urate nephropathy. Drug classification: (A) for acute gout — NSAIDs (naproxen, indomethacin, diclofenac, etoricoxib; not aspirin), colchicine, corticosteroids (oral prednisolone, intra-articular), IL-1 blockers (anakinra, canakinumab); (B) for chronic gout (urate-lowering therapy) — synthesis inhibitors: xanthine oxidase inhibitors allopurinol and febuxostat; uricosurics: probenecid, sulfinpyrazone, lesinurad, benzbromarone; uricolytics (recombinant uricase): rasburicase, pegloticase. Colchicine (from Colchicum autumnale): binds tubulin, preventing microtubule polymerisation, thereby inhibiting neutrophil migration, phagocytosis of urate crystals, release of glycoprotein (leukotactic) and NLRP3 inflammasome activation; not analgesic or uricosuric; specific for gout; used in acute gout (1.2 mg then 0.6 mg after 1 h — low-dose regimen), prophylaxis during initiation of urate-lowering therapy (0.5–0.6 mg once or twice daily), familial Mediterranean fever, pericarditis, Behçet disease; adverse effects — nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhoea (dose-limiting), myopathy, neuropathy, agranulocytosis, alopecia; toxic in overdose. Allopurinol: purine analogue, competitively inhibits xanthine oxidase (and its metabolite alloxanthine/oxypurinol is a non-competitive inhibitor with long t½), reducing uric acid production; increases hypoxanthine and xanthine (more soluble); started at low dose (100 mg/day) after acute attack subsides, titrated to target serum urate < 6 mg/dL (< 5 with tophi), with colchicine prophylaxis for 3–6 months; uses — chronic tophaceous gout, recurrent attacks, urate nephropathy and stones, prevention of tumour lysis syndrome, Lesch–Nyhan syndrome, kala-azar (adjunct), calcium oxalate stones with hyperuricosuria; adverse effects — acute gout flare on initiation, skin rashes, hypersensitivity syndrome, Stevens–Johnson syndrome/TEN (risk with HLA-B*5801 — common in Asians; screen), hepatotoxicity, GI upset; interactions — inhibits metabolism of azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine (reduce their dose to one-quarter), potentiates warfarin, rash with ampicillin. Febuxostat: non-purine selective XO inhibitor; used when allopurinol not tolerated; cardiovascular warning. Probenecid: inhibits URAT1-mediated urate reabsorption; ineffective in renal impairment; requires high fluid intake; also prolongs penicillin action. Rasburicase: converts uric acid to allantoin; used in tumour lysis syndrome; contraindicated in G6PD deficiency. General measures: weight reduction, limit alcohol, purine-rich foods and fructose drinks, avoid thiazides and low-dose aspirin where possible, losartan and fenofibrate have mild uricosuric effect.",
[["Classification",[["Phase","Drugs"],["Acute attack","NSAIDs, colchicine, corticosteroids, IL-1 blockers"],["XO inhibitors","Allopurinol, febuxostat"],["Uricosurics","Probenecid, lesinurad, benzbromarone"],["Uricases","Rasburicase, pegloticase"]]],
["Colchicine vs allopurinol",[["Feature","Colchicine","Allopurinol"],["Mechanism","Tubulin binding — ↓ neutrophil activity","Xanthine oxidase inhibition"],["Use","Acute gout, prophylaxis","Chronic gout, urate lowering"],["Effect on uric acid","None","↓ Production"],["ADR","Diarrhoea, myopathy, marrow suppression","Rash, SJS, initial flare"],["Interaction","CYP3A4 inhibitors ↑ toxicity","↑ Azathioprine/6-MP toxicity"]]]],
{c:"Acute gout responds to anti-inflammatory drugs; long-term control needs urate-lowering therapy, started after the attack with prophylactic cover and titrated to target urate levels."});

Z("pharm3",6,"ph",["Cough is treated according to type: dry cough with antitussives (suppressants) and productive cough with expectorants or mucolytics like bromhexine.","Dry cough is a useless alarm that can be quietened; productive cough is a useful cleaning action — help it by thinning the mucus rather than suppressing it.",["Antitussives: opioids (codeine, pholcodine), non-opioids (dextromethorphan, noscapine), antihistamines (chlorpheniramine, diphenhydramine), peripherally acting (benzonatate).","Expectorants: secretory (guaiphenesin, ammonium chloride, potassium iodide, vasaka), mucolytics (bromhexine, ambroxol, acetylcysteine, carbocisteine).","Bromhexine: derivative of vasicine (Adhatoda vasica); depolymerises mucopolysaccharides, ↑ lysosomal enzymes → thin sputum; ambroxol is its active metabolite; ADR — rhinorrhoea, lacrimation, GI upset.","Pharyngeal demulcents (lozenges, honey), bronchodilators; treat underlying cause; avoid cough suppressants in productive cough and children < 2 years."],"Dry cough = suppress (dextromethorphan); wet cough = thin it (bromhexine)."],
"Cough is a protective reflex that clears the airways; symptomatic treatment depends on its nature and cause. Classification of drugs: (1) pharyngeal demulcents — lozenges, cough drops, linctuses with syrup, glycerine, liquorice, honey: soothe irritated pharyngeal mucosa; (2) expectorants (mucokinetics) — (a) bronchial secretion enhancers: sodium or potassium citrate, potassium iodide, guaiphenesin (glyceryl guaiacolate), ammonium chloride, vasaka (Adhatoda vasica) — increase volume of thinner secretions, reflexly via gastric irritation; (b) mucolytics: bromhexine, ambroxol, acetylcysteine, carbocisteine, dornase alfa (cystic fibrosis), hypertonic saline; (3) antitussives (cough suppressants) — (a) opioids: codeine, ethylmorphine, pholcodine; (b) non-opioids: noscapine, dextromethorphan, chlophedianol; (c) antihistamines: chlorpheniramine, diphenhydramine, promethazine (sedative, anticholinergic); (d) peripherally acting: benzonatate, levodropropizine; (4) adjuncts — bronchodilators (salbutamol, ipratropium) when bronchospasm; inhaled steroids (asthma); PPIs (GERD cough); antibiotics (bacterial infection). Bromhexine: a semisynthetic derivative of the alkaloid vasicine; acts as mucolytic by depolymerising mucopolysaccharides directly and by liberating lysosomal enzymes in goblet cells, breaking down the fibre network of tenacious sputum; it also increases secretion of thin serous mucus and enhances mucociliary clearance; dose 8–16 mg TDS orally; useful when sputum is thick and difficult to expectorate (chronic bronchitis, bronchiectasis, COPD); adverse effects — rhinorrhoea, lacrimation, gastric irritation, hypersensitivity; ambroxol is its active metabolite (also used in neonatal RDS earlier). Principles: identify and treat underlying cause (infection, asthma, GERD, ACE-inhibitor cough, post-nasal drip, smoking, TB); suppressants are appropriate for dry, unproductive, distressing cough (e.g., night-time cough, pertussis) but should not be used in productive cough or asthma; codeine causes constipation, drowsiness and dependence and is contraindicated in children under 12 years; many irrational cough FDCs are banned; dextromethorphan acts centrally on NMDA/sigma receptors, non-addictive at therapeutic doses but abused at high doses.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs","Use"],["Demulcents","Lozenges, honey, glycerine","Pharyngeal irritation"],["Expectorants","Guaiphenesin, potassium citrate, vasaka","Thick secretions"],["Mucolytics","Bromhexine, ambroxol, acetylcysteine, carbocisteine","Tenacious sputum"],["Antitussives — opioid","Codeine, pholcodine","Dry cough"],["Antitussives — non-opioid","Dextromethorphan, noscapine","Dry cough"],["Antihistamines","Chlorpheniramine, promethazine","Allergic/nocturnal cough"]]],
["Bromhexine",["Derived from vasicine (Adhatoda vasica)","Mucolytic: depolymerises mucopolysaccharides, ↑ lysosomal enzymes","Dose 8–16 mg TDS","ADR: rhinorrhoea, lacrimation, GI upset","Ambroxol = active metabolite"]]],
{c:"Rational cough therapy treats the cause, suppresses only dry unproductive cough and uses mucolytics such as bromhexine to help clear thick sputum."});

Z("pharm3",7,"ph",["Drugs for bronchial asthma either relieve bronchospasm quickly (relievers like salbutamol) or control airway inflammation long-term (controllers like inhaled steroids); inhaled therapy is preferred.","Relievers are fire extinguishers for an attack; controllers are fire-prevention systems that calm the inflamed airways day after day.",["Bronchodilators: β2 agonists — SABA (salbutamol, terbutaline), LABA (salmeterol, formoterol); anticholinergics (ipratropium, tiotropium); methylxanthines (theophylline, aminophylline, doxofylline).","Anti-inflammatory: inhaled corticosteroids (budesonide, fluticasone, beclomethasone — cornerstone), systemic steroids; leukotriene antagonists (montelukast, zafirlukast); mast cell stabilisers (cromolyn); anti-IgE (omalizumab), anti-IL-5 (mepolizumab), anti-IL-4R (dupilumab).","Salbutamol: β2 agonist → ↑ cAMP → bronchodilation; inhaled acts in 5 min; ADR — tremor, tachycardia, hypokalaemia. Methylxanthines: PDE inhibition + adenosine antagonism; narrow therapeutic index (5–15 µg/mL) — arrhythmia, seizures; many interactions.","Montelukast: CysLT1 antagonist — exercise and aspirin-induced asthma, allergic rhinitis; steroids reduce inflammation, ↑ β2 receptor expression; inhaled ADR — oral candidiasis, dysphonia (rinse mouth, spacer). GINA: ICS–formoterol as reliever and step-up."],"Asthma: 'Reliever = SABA; Controller = ICS'."],
"Bronchial asthma is chronic airway inflammation with reversible airflow obstruction and hyperresponsiveness; drugs either relieve bronchospasm or control inflammation. Classification: (I) bronchodilators — (a) β2 sympathomimetics: short-acting (salbutamol, terbutaline, levosalbutamol) and long-acting (salmeterol, formoterol, bambuterol; ultra-long indacaterol, vilanterol, olodaterol); (b) methylxanthines: theophylline, aminophylline, doxofylline, acebrophylline; (c) anticholinergics: ipratropium bromide (short-acting), tiotropium (long-acting); (II) leukotriene modifiers — receptor antagonists (montelukast, zafirlukast), 5-lipoxygenase inhibitor (zileuton); (III) mast cell stabilisers — sodium cromoglycate, ketotifen; (IV) corticosteroids — inhaled (beclomethasone, budesonide, fluticasone, ciclesonide, mometasone), systemic (prednisolone, methylprednisolone, hydrocortisone); (V) biologicals — anti-IgE (omalizumab), anti-IL-5 (mepolizumab, reslizumab), anti-IL-5R (benralizumab), anti-IL-4Rα (dupilumab), anti-TSLP (tezepelumab). Salbutamol: selective β2 agonist; activates adenylyl cyclase via Gs, raising cAMP, which relaxes bronchial smooth muscle (also inhibits mediator release from mast cells, increases mucociliary clearance); by inhalation (MDI, DPI, nebuliser) acts within 5 minutes, lasts 4–6 hours; orally and IV also; uses — acute relief of asthma and COPD symptoms, exercise-induced bronchospasm prevention, severe acute asthma (nebulised), hyperkalaemia (shifts K⁺ into cells), premature labour (tocolysis, not preferred); adverse effects — muscle tremor (commonest), palpitations, tachycardia, restlessness, hypokalaemia, hyperglycaemia, tolerance with regular use; regular SABA monotherapy is not advised (GINA). Methylxanthines (theophylline): mechanisms — inhibition of phosphodiesterase (↑ cAMP), adenosine A1/A2 receptor antagonism, histone deacetylase activation (enhancing steroid action), increased diaphragmatic contractility; actions — bronchodilatation, CNS stimulation, cardiac stimulation, diuresis, gastric secretion; narrow therapeutic range 5–15 µg/mL; metabolised by CYP1A2 with saturation kinetics; levels increased by erythromycin, ciprofloxacin, cimetidine, allopurinol, heart failure, liver disease; reduced by smoking, rifampicin, phenytoin; adverse effects — nausea, vomiting, headache, insomnia, tremor, tachycardia, arrhythmias, seizures; uses — add-on in asthma/COPD (sustained-release), apnoea of prematurity (caffeine preferred), aminophylline IV in acute severe asthma (rarely now). Leukotriene antagonists (montelukast 10 mg once daily at night): block CysLT1 receptor, preventing bronchoconstriction, mucus secretion, oedema and eosinophil recruitment by LTC4/D4/E4; less effective than ICS; uses — mild persistent asthma (alternative to ICS), add-on, exercise-induced and aspirin-exacerbated asthma, allergic rhinitis; adverse — headache, GI upset, neuropsychiatric effects (warning), rarely Churg–Strauss. Corticosteroids: reduce airway inflammation by inhibiting cytokine (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13) production, eosinophil recruitment, mast cell numbers, mediator synthesis (via lipocortin inhibiting PLA2), microvascular leakage, upregulating β2 receptors; inhaled steroids are the most effective controllers and reduce exacerbations and deaths; adverse effects of ICS — oropharyngeal candidiasis, hoarseness/dysphonia (use spacer, rinse mouth), minimal systemic effects at usual doses (growth slowing in children, adrenal suppression, osteoporosis, cataract at high doses); systemic steroids for acute exacerbations (prednisolone 40–50 mg for 5–7 days) and severe asthma. Stepwise management (GINA 2024): track 1 — as-needed low-dose ICS–formoterol at steps 1–2, maintenance and reliever therapy (MART) with ICS–formoterol at steps 3–5; add LAMA, biologics, low-dose OCS at step 5; inhaler technique and adherence are essential.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["SABA","Salbutamol, terbutaline, levosalbutamol"],["LABA","Salmeterol, formoterol, indacaterol"],["Methylxanthines","Theophylline, aminophylline, doxofylline"],["Anticholinergics","Ipratropium, tiotropium"],["Leukotriene modifiers","Montelukast, zafirlukast, zileuton"],["Mast cell stabilisers","Cromolyn, ketotifen"],["Corticosteroids","Budesonide, fluticasone, beclomethasone; prednisolone"],["Biologicals","Omalizumab, mepolizumab, benralizumab, dupilumab"]]],
["Key drugs",[["Drug","Mechanism","Key ADR"],["Salbutamol","β2 → ↑ cAMP","Tremor, tachycardia, hypokalaemia"],["Theophylline","PDE inhibition, adenosine antagonism","Arrhythmia, seizures, interactions"],["Montelukast","CysLT1 antagonist","Headache, neuropsychiatric"],["Inhaled steroids","Anti-inflammatory (gene transcription)","Oral thrush, dysphonia"]]],
["Inhaled vs oral",["Inhalation: rapid, high local, low systemic dose","Devices: MDI with spacer, DPI, nebuliser","Correct technique crucial"]]],
{i:"Asthma control depends on suppressing airway inflammation; inhaled corticosteroids reduce exacerbations and mortality, while bronchodilators provide symptom relief.",x:[["GINA step approach (simplified)",["Steps 1–2: as-needed low-dose ICS–formoterol","Step 3: low-dose maintenance ICS–formoterol (MART)","Step 4: medium-dose ICS–formoterol","Step 5: add LAMA, refer for phenotyping, biologics"]]],f:"Allergen → mast cell (IgE) → histamine, LTs → bronchospasm, oedema, mucus\n Relievers: SABA (↑cAMP), ipratropium (↓ACh), theophylline (PDE)\n Controllers: ICS (↓ inflammation), montelukast (block LTs), biologics (anti-IgE, IL-5)",c:"Asthma pharmacotherapy combines anti-inflammatory controllers, chiefly inhaled steroids, with bronchodilators for relief; inhalation maximises benefit and minimises toxicity."});

Z("pharm3",8,"ph",["Status asthmaticus (acute severe asthma) is a life-threatening attack not responding to usual bronchodilators; it needs oxygen, repeated nebulised salbutamol and ipratropium, systemic steroids, and IV magnesium.","An emergency where the airway is clamped shut: open it with repeated inhalers, cool the inflammation with steroids, and support breathing with oxygen.",["Signs: inability to speak sentences, RR > 25–30, HR > 110–120, PEF < 50%, silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion, confusion — life-threatening.","Oxygen to SpO2 93–95%; nebulised salbutamol 5 mg every 20 min (or continuous) + ipratropium 0.5 mg.","Systemic steroids: hydrocortisone 100–200 mg IV 6-hourly or prednisolone 40–50 mg orally.","IV magnesium sulphate 2 g over 20 min; consider IV aminophylline/salbutamol, adrenaline (anaphylaxis); avoid sedatives; ICU, ventilation if failing; no routine antibiotics."],"Status asthmaticus 'O SHIT M': Oxygen, Salbutamol, Hydrocortisone, Ipratropium, Theophylline, Magnesium."],
"Status asthmaticus (acute severe asthma) is a severe exacerbation unresponsive to standard bronchodilator therapy, which may progress to respiratory failure and death. Features: breathlessness at rest, unable to complete sentences, respiratory rate > 25–30/min, heart rate > 110–120/min, pulsus paradoxus, use of accessory muscles, PEF < 50% of best/predicted; life-threatening — PEF < 33%, SpO2 < 92%, silent chest, cyanosis, poor respiratory effort, hypotension, arrhythmia, exhaustion, altered consciousness, normal or rising PaCO2. Management (in hospital/emergency): sit upright; oxygen to maintain SpO2 93–95% (94–98% in some guidelines); nebulised salbutamol 2.5–5 mg (or terbutaline) every 15–20 minutes for the first hour or continuously (oxygen-driven) or by MDI with spacer (4–10 puffs); add nebulised ipratropium bromide 0.5 mg every 4–6 hours (synergistic); systemic corticosteroids early — prednisolone 40–50 mg orally or hydrocortisone 100–200 mg IV every 6 hours (effects in 4–6 h, continued 5–7 days); IV magnesium sulphate 1.2–2 g over 20 minutes for severe/life-threatening asthma not responding (bronchodilator via calcium antagonism); consider IV aminophylline (5 mg/kg loading if not on theophylline, then 0.5 mg/kg/h with level monitoring) or IV salbutamol in refractory cases; IV fluids and potassium supplementation (β2 agonists and steroids cause hypokalaemia); antibiotics only if bacterial infection; avoid sedatives and hypnotics (respiratory depression), and β-blockers; no role for mucolytics or routine chest physiotherapy; arterial blood gases, chest X-ray to exclude pneumothorax; ICU admission with non-invasive or invasive mechanical ventilation (permissive hypercapnia), ketamine or inhalational anaesthetics for refractory bronchospasm; heliox in selected patients. After recovery — continue ICS, oral steroids course, review technique, written action plan.",
[["Management",[["Step","Drug/measure","Dose"],["Oxygen","Target SpO2 93–95%","Controlled"],["SABA","Salbutamol nebulised","2.5–5 mg every 20 min"],["Anticholinergic","Ipratropium nebulised","0.5 mg 4–6 hourly"],["Steroid","Hydrocortisone IV / prednisolone oral","100–200 mg 6 hourly / 40–50 mg daily"],["Magnesium","MgSO4 IV","2 g over 20 min"],["Refractory","Aminophylline IV, IV salbutamol, ventilation","With monitoring"]]],
["Avoid",["Sedatives, opioids","β-blockers","Routine antibiotics","Mucolytics"]]],
{c:"Acute severe asthma is managed aggressively with oxygen, repeated inhaled bronchodilators, early systemic steroids and IV magnesium, escalating to ventilation if needed."});

Z("pharm3",9,"ph",["Serotonin (5-HT) drugs act on many receptor types: agonists like triptans (5-HT1B/1D) treat migraine; antagonists like ondansetron (5-HT3) stop vomiting and cyproheptadine (5-HT2) treats serotonin syndrome and stimulates appetite.","Serotonin is a messenger with many doors; each drug opens or closes a specific door to get a targeted effect.",["5-HT1A partial agonist: buspirone (anxiety). 5-HT1B/1D agonists: sumatriptan (migraine). 5-HT4 agonists: prucalopride (constipation).","5-HT3 antagonists: ondansetron, granisetron, palonosetron — chemotherapy/radiation/post-op vomiting; ADR — headache, constipation, QT prolongation.","5-HT2 antagonists: cyproheptadine (also H1) — serotonin syndrome, appetite stimulation, carcinoid; ketanserin; atypical antipsychotics (5-HT2A).","Others: SSRIs (↑ 5-HT), MAOIs, ergot alkaloids, methysergide, LSD (5-HT2A agonist)."],"Ondansetron = 'On-dan-SET-ron for 5-HT3: Stops Emesis'."],
"5-Hydroxytryptamine (serotonin) acts through seven receptor families (5-HT1–7); all are GPCRs except 5-HT3, a ligand-gated cation channel. Drugs acting on serotonergic system: agonists — 5-HT1A: buspirone (partial agonist; anxiolytic without sedation), gepirone; 5-HT1B/1D: triptans (sumatriptan, rizatriptan, zolmitriptan) for acute migraine — vasoconstriction of cranial vessels and inhibition of CGRP release; 5-HT1F: lasmiditan (migraine); 5-HT2C: lorcaserin (obesity, withdrawn); 5-HT4: prucalopride (chronic constipation), cisapride (withdrawn — QT), mosapride, tegaserod; metoclopramide partially; antagonists — 5-HT2A/2C: cyproheptadine (also H1 and anticholinergic), ketanserin, methysergide (migraine prophylaxis; retroperitoneal fibrosis), pizotifen, atypical antipsychotics (clozapine, risperidone, olanzapine), mirtazapine, trazodone; 5-HT3: ondansetron, granisetron, dolasetron, palonosetron (longest-acting; prevents delayed CINV), alosetron (IBS-D); drugs affecting serotonin levels — SSRIs (fluoxetine, sertraline), SNRIs, TCAs (reuptake inhibition), MAO inhibitors, tryptophan, reserpine (depletes), fenfluramine (releases); ergot alkaloids (partial agonists/antagonists); hallucinogens (LSD — 5-HT2A agonist). Ondansetron: selective 5-HT3 antagonist acting on vagal afferents in the gut and chemoreceptor trigger zone/nucleus tractus solitarius; highly effective against chemotherapy-induced (especially acute, with dexamethasone and NK1 antagonist aprepitant), radiotherapy-induced, postoperative nausea and vomiting, hyperemesis gravidarum (second-line), gastroenteritis vomiting in children; ineffective in motion sickness; given orally, IV or as orally disintegrating tablet; adverse effects — headache, constipation, dizziness, transient rise in liver enzymes, QT prolongation (avoid high IV doses), serotonin syndrome with serotonergic drugs. Cyproheptadine: 5-HT2 and H1 antagonist with anticholinergic and calcium channel–blocking activity; uses — allergic conditions (pruritus, urticaria), appetite stimulation and weight gain, serotonin syndrome (antidote), carcinoid syndrome (diarrhoea), migraine prophylaxis in children, postgastrectomy dumping syndrome; adverse effects — sedation, dry mouth, weight gain. Triptans are covered under migraine.",
[["5-HT receptor drugs",[["Receptor","Agonist","Antagonist"],["5-HT1A","Buspirone","—"],["5-HT1B/1D","Sumatriptan","—"],["5-HT2A/2C","LSD","Cyproheptadine, ketanserin, atypical antipsychotics"],["5-HT3","—","Ondansetron, granisetron, palonosetron"],["5-HT4","Prucalopride, cisapride, mosapride","—"]]],
["Ondansetron uses",["Chemotherapy-induced nausea/vomiting (with dexamethasone ± aprepitant)","Radiotherapy-induced vomiting","Postoperative nausea and vomiting","Gastroenteritis vomiting in children","Not effective in motion sickness"]]],
{c:"Serotonergic drugs target specific receptor subtypes — triptans for migraine, setrons for vomiting and cyproheptadine for allergy, appetite and serotonin syndrome."});

Z("pharm3",10,"ph",["Paracetamol (acetaminophen) is a safe analgesic and antipyretic at normal doses but causes severe liver damage in overdose, which is treated with N-acetylcysteine.","At normal doses the liver safely neutralises paracetamol; in overdose its detox stores (glutathione) run out and a toxic metabolite (NAPQI) burns liver cells. NAC refills the detox stores.",["MOA: weak peripheral COX inhibition; central COX (COX-3?) inhibition, serotonergic and cannabinoid pathways — analgesic, antipyretic, poor anti-inflammatory.","Advantages: no GI irritation, no antiplatelet effect, safe in pregnancy, children, peptic ulcer, asthma.","Toxicity: > 150 mg/kg or > 10 g in adults; NAPQI (CYP2E1) depletes glutathione → centrilobular hepatic necrosis; risk ↑ in alcoholics, enzyme induction, malnutrition.","Antidote: N-acetylcysteine (best within 8–10 h; nomogram — Rumack–Matthew); activated charcoal within 1 h; liver transplant if fulminant failure."],"Paracetamol poisoning: 'NAPQI → glutathione gone → NAC to rescue'."],
"Paracetamol (acetaminophen) is a para-aminophenol derivative with good analgesic and antipyretic but negligible anti-inflammatory activity. Mechanism: weak inhibition of COX-1 and COX-2 in peripheral tissues (high peroxide environment of inflammation negates it), more effective inhibition in the CNS (reducing PGE2 in hypothalamus — antipyretic), activation of descending serotonergic pathways, and metabolite AM404 acting on cannabinoid CB1 and TRPV1 receptors. It does not affect platelets, uric acid excretion, respiration or acid–base balance and causes little gastric irritation. Pharmacokinetics: rapidly absorbed orally (also IV, rectal), uniformly distributed, minimal protein binding, t½ 2–3 h; metabolised in liver mainly by glucuronidation and sulphation; a small fraction (5–10%) is oxidised by CYP2E1 (and 3A4, 1A2) to the reactive metabolite N-acetyl-p-benzoquinoneimine (NAPQI), which is normally detoxified by conjugation with glutathione. Uses: analgesic for headache, toothache, myalgia, dysmenorrhoea, osteoarthritis (first-line), post-operative pain (IV); antipyretic of choice, especially in children, pregnancy, peptic ulcer, bleeding disorders, aspirin-sensitive asthma, dengue (avoid NSAIDs). Dose: adults 0.5–1 g every 6–8 h (maximum 4 g/day; 2–3 g in alcoholics and liver disease); children 10–15 mg/kg every 4–6 h. Adverse effects: rare at therapeutic doses — nausea, rashes, rarely thrombocytopenia, hepatotoxicity with chronic excess, nephrotoxicity with chronic use. Acute toxicity: occurs with ingestion > 150 mg/kg (> 7.5–10 g in adults); glucuronidation and sulphation saturate, more NAPQI is formed, glutathione is depleted (< 30%), and NAPQI binds covalently to hepatic proteins, causing centrilobular (zone 3) hepatic necrosis and acute tubular necrosis. Risk increased in chronic alcoholics, those on enzyme inducers (rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, isoniazid), malnourished and fasting patients (low glutathione). Clinical course: stage I (0–24 h) — nausea, vomiting, anorexia, sweating, or asymptomatic; stage II (24–72 h) — RUQ pain, rising transaminases; stage III (72–96 h) — hepatic failure, jaundice, coagulopathy, hypoglycaemia, encephalopathy, renal failure, lactic acidosis, death; stage IV — recovery. Management: activated charcoal if within 1–2 hours; measure plasma paracetamol level at ≥ 4 hours and plot on the Rumack–Matthew nomogram (treatment line 150 µg/mL at 4 h, or 100 µg/mL in UK); antidote N-acetylcysteine — replenishes glutathione (precursor of cysteine), directly conjugates NAPQI, and improves hepatic microcirculation; most effective within 8–10 hours but beneficial even later; IV regimen (21-hour: 150 mg/kg over 1 h, 50 mg/kg over 4 h, 100 mg/kg over 16 h) or oral (140 mg/kg loading then 70 mg/kg every 4 h × 17 doses); adverse effects of IV NAC — anaphylactoid reactions; methionine (oral) is an alternative; supportive care for liver failure; liver transplantation if King's College criteria met (pH < 7.3, INR > 6.5, creatinine > 3.4 mg/dL, grade III/IV encephalopathy).",
[["Pharmacology",["Analgesic, antipyretic; poor anti-inflammatory","Central COX inhibition, serotonergic, AM404 (CB1)","No GI irritation, no antiplatelet effect","Safe in pregnancy, children, peptic ulcer, dengue","Max 4 g/day"]],
["Toxicity mechanism",["Saturation of glucuronidation and sulphation","↑ NAPQI via CYP2E1","Glutathione depletion","Covalent binding → centrilobular necrosis"]],
["Management of overdose",[["Step","Detail"],["Decontamination","Activated charcoal within 1–2 h"],["Assess","Plasma level ≥ 4 h; Rumack–Matthew nomogram"],["Antidote","N-acetylcysteine IV/oral (best < 8–10 h)"],["Supportive","Glucose, FFP, treat encephalopathy"],["Transplant","King's College criteria"]]]],
{c:"Paracetamol is the safest common analgesic–antipyretic at therapeutic doses, but overdose causes dose-dependent hepatotoxicity preventable by timely N-acetylcysteine."});
